  "strategy": "We introduce a novel multi-resolution optimization approach with targeted local search and continuous-refinement phases. Unlike previous attempts, we will use a hybrid representation allowing both binary and continuous variables, projected back to binary while respecting the integral constraint. The core innovation is a two-phase optimization: (1) Simulated Annealing with binary moves but continuous feasibility via projection, and (2) Gradient descent on the continuous representation while maintaining binary projection. \n\nWe'll maintain the integral constraint using a repair operator that flips least-significant bits while preserving local structure. The algorithm will explore multiple resolutions (400, 800, 1600, 3200 points) and validate all candidates with the original correlation function. The search will compare:\n\n- Repair effectiveness: Flip least significant bits rather than arbitrary random ones\n- Multi-resolution passes: Starting from coarse to fine grids\n- Dynamic temperature cooling: Faster decay schedule than previous implementations\n\nFor the continuous refinement phase, we'll use projected gradients with adaptive step sizes. The repair operator ensures strictly binary outputs while the continuous refinement allows faster escape from local optima.\n\nThe implementation includes:\n1. Projected simulated annealing with temperature decay\n2. Continuous gradient descent with projection\n3. Multi-resolution adaptive search\n4. Integral constraint repair\n5. Objective function using FFT correlation\n\nThe CF was scaled initially from the provided parent solution and then submitted to multi-resolution optimization. The best candidate was found using a binary representation projected back from continuous coordinates.\n\nLet me know if you need further details.",
